    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go a bit deeper into the downstream effects of that decreased aldosterone? Like, what specifically happens at the level of the kidneys and electrolytes when that part of the pathway is inhibited?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When ACE inhibitors inhibit angiotensin II levels, the decrease in aldosterone release leads to decreased sodium reabsorption in the kidneys. Specifically, aldosterone stimulates the kidneys to reabsorb sodium ions in exchange for excreting potassium ions. With decreased aldosterone, the kidneys excrete more potassium, which can lead to increased potassium levels in the bloodstream (hyperkalemia). Additionally, the kidneys reabsorb less sodium, resulting in increased urinary sodium excretion and a mild diuretic effect, which contributes to blood pressure lowering.",
      "source": "target",
